    method_intro:'Étude isn’t a quiz — it’s built on what cognitive science actually says about learning a skill. Six ideas do the heavy lifting. None of them involve studying harder; they involve studying smarter.',
    m1n:'Spaced repetition', m1d:'You remember something best when you review it right as you’re about to forget it. Each successful recall lets the next review wait a little longer.', m1a:'every concept carries its own due-date. Today shows what’s due; the schedule stretches as you succeed and snaps back when you slip.',
    m2n:'Retrieval practice', m2d:'Pulling an answer out of memory strengthens it far more than putting it back in by re-reading. This is the “testing effect.”', m2a:'you always produce the answer — play the interval, build the chord, name what you hear — never just look at a chart.',
    m3n:'Interleaving', m3d:'Mixing different problem types in one sitting feels harder than doing them in blocks — and that’s exactly why it builds more durable, flexible memory.', m3a:'a session shuffles intervals, chords, scales and progressions together, never two of a kind back to back.',
    m4n:'Desirable difficulty', m4d:'A little struggle is the signal that learning is happening. Effortless review barely moves the needle.', m4a:'once you can play a concept, it graduates to by ear — and the key changes every time, so you learn the idea, not one fingering.',
